    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.reachability_index - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.reachability_index.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.reachability_index()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Build an index that answers reachability queries without searching the\n    graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.reachability_index()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Build an index that answers reachability queries without searching the\n    graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.reachability_index()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Build an index that answers reachability queries without searching the graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nBuild an index that answers reachability queries without searching the graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L60864-L60975){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.reachability_index()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Reachability index](../assets/graphs/reachability-index.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a seven-node directed graph in which the cycle A, B, C (shaded) collapses to one component with an edge to D; G also points to D, and D points to E and F. D has the most edges in and out, so it becomes the first hub (teal): it joins the out-list of every component that reaches it and the in-list of every component it reaches. Right: the query index(A, F) is True because A's out-list and F's in-list share the hub D (the path through D in the accent color), and index(F, A) is False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\n`has_path()` searches the graph on every call, which adds up when the same fixed graph\nis asked \"can `u` reach `v`?\" thousands of times (dependency, provenance or citation\ngraphs, for instance). This method does the work once: it collapses each strongly\nconnected component to a single node and gives every component two short lists of\n*hub* components, the hubs it reaches and the hubs that reach it. Then `u` reaches `v`\nexactly when they share a component or a hub of `u`'s first list is in `v`'s second.\n\nThe returned function answers `index(u, v)` with the same result as\n`has_path(u, v)`. On an undirected graph the components are the connected components,\nso every query reduces to a component comparison.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Callable]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A function `index(u, v)` returning `True` if `v` is reachable from `u` (every node\n    reaches itself). It takes IDs or labels. Its `n_components` attribute is the number\n    of components and `label_size` the total number of hub entries over all lists.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   When called with a node that does not exist, or one added after the index was built\n    (rebuild the index after changing the graph).\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nA 2-hop reachability cover built by pruned landmark labeling (Akiba, Iwata and\nYoshida, 2013; adapted to reachability by Yano et al., 2013), on the condensation.\nComponents are processed in decreasing order of $(\\deg^- + 1)(\\deg^+ + 1)$ in the\ncondensation (ties go to the lower Tarjan component index). Each one runs a forward\nBFS that adds it to the in-list of every component it reaches, and a backward BFS that\nadds it to the out-list of every component reaching it; a branch is pruned as soon as\nthe hubs already placed answer the query. On graphs with high-degree hubs this keeps\nthe lists short; on long chains, where nearly every component ties on the degree\nscore, the lists grow quadratically (about 20,000 entries for a 200-node path).\nBuilding takes $O(V + E)$ for the condensation plus the pruned searches (at most\n$O(C (C + E_C))$ for $C$ components and $E_C$ condensation edges); a query merges two\nsorted lists. The index is a snapshot of the graph. This follows NetworkX's proposed\n`pll_reachability_graph` (PR #8718, not yet merged); see Zhang, Bonifati and Özsu,\n*Indexing Techniques for Graph Reachability Queries*, for a survey.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~has_path`) \n\n:   Answer a single reachability question.\n\n[](`~transitive_closure`) \n\n:   A graph with an edge for every reachable pair.\n\n[](`~descendants`) \n\n:   Every node reachable from a node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA cycle A, B, C collapses to one component, which reaches D but is not reached from it:\n\n::: {#3065a5f9 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")], directed=True\n)\ngraph.add_node(label=\"E\")\n\nindex = graph.reachability_index()\nindex(\"B\", \"A\"), index(\"A\", \"D\"), index(\"D\", \"A\"), index(\"A\", \"E\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, True, False, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Which courses need a course\n\n`courses()` links each course of a made-up degree program to the courses that require it.\nBuilding the index once makes each question about the prerequisites a quick lookup: a\nstudent who has not taken MATH101 cannot take CS360, but can take CS410:\n\n::: {#57f136ac .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncourses = ec.courses()\nindex = courses.reachability_index()\n\nindex(\"MATH101\", \"CS360\"), index(\"MATH101\", \"CS410\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEvery course reaches itself, so asking the index about every course gives MATH101 and the\nsix courses it leads to:\n\n::: {#1ef10696 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nneeds_math101 = [n for n in courses.get_node_ids() if index(\"MATH101\", n)]\ncourses.plot(highlight=needs_math101, label_by=\"label\", seed=2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, ht